From the first equation, we have $x = y^{3/2}$.
Substituting this into the second equation, we have
\begin{align*} (y - y^{3/2})^2 &= 4y^2 \\ y^2 - 2y^{5/2} + y^3 &= 4y^2 \\ y^3 - 3y^2 + 2y^{5/2} &= 0 \\ y^3 - 3y^2 + 2y^{5/2} &= 0 \\ y^2(y - 3) + 2y^{5/2} &= 0 \\ y^2(y - 3) + 2y^{5/2} &= 0\end{align*}
Since $y$ is a positive real number, we can divide by $y^{5/2}$ to get
\begin{align*} y^{3/2} - 3y^{1/2} + 2 &= 0 \\ y^{3/2} - 3\sqrt{y} + 2 &= 0\end{align*}
Let $z = \sqrt{y}$. Then $z^3 - 3z + 2 = 0$. We can factor this as
\begin{align*} z^3 - 3z + 2 &= (z - 1)(z^2 + z - 2) = (z - 1)(z - 1)(z + 2) = (z - 1)^2 (z + 2) = 0\end{align*}
Since $y$ is positive, $z = \sqrt{y} > 0$, so we must have $z = 1$. Thus, $\sqrt{y} = 1$, which means $y = 1$.
